search: slice the index walks and build the trie before answering, fixes F-010

From: Andy Green <andy@warmcat.com>

The extent walk and the full-text index walk both ran unyielded in a
single task_function() slice, so unauthenticated search requests on
large unindexed repos pinned threadpool workers for the whole build,
and STOPPING (client gone) was only observable at task entry.

- Both walks now run from job_search() in bounded slices of 128 tree
  entries (checked at the top of each iteration so whitelist-skip
  'continue' paths cannot unbound a slice), returning 0 with
  ctx->partway set by the caller and resuming from ctx->stack.  Worker
  slices are ms-scale and STOPPING is honoured at every slice boundary.
- The creator of a missing trie now builds it first and answers with
  real results, instead of sending a {creating:...} stub and trying to
  continue in the background.  Background continuation never survived
  the stub: the task is dequeued by HTTP_DROP_PROTOCOL as soon as its
  HTTP transaction completes.  A 2019 change had also made the stub
  path destroy the ctx, which unlinked the ongoing registration and
  left the trie permanently unbuildable, re-creating a half-built temp
  per request.  Concurrent clients during the build still get the
  {ongoing:...} progress response from the existing path.
- Extend the 503 shed heuristic to first-touch search URLs (search, ac,
  fp modes and search= query args), so a crawl forcing index builds of
  every hosted repo cannot monopolize the pool; already-indexed search
  still runs when the pool has headroom.
